WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.489
A pointer is not the same thing as a bounded array.

2
00:00:03.489 --> 00:00:12.054
A pointer may identify an element or the beginning of a range, but it does not inherently tell the callee how many elements are valid, where the

3
00:00:12.054 --> 00:00:17.446
range starts, where it ends, who owns the storage, or how long the object will remain alive.

4
00:00:17.446 --> 00:00:21.253
Raw arrays often lose useful information when they cross a function boundary.

5
00:00:21.253 --> 00:00:29.817
In many expressions, an array decays to a pointer, and the extent that was visible at the declaration site is no longer present in the parameter type.

6
00:00:29.817 --> 00:00:34.893
The receiving function may know an address, but not the valid range that address belongs to.

7
00:00:34.893 --> 00:00:39.651
Pointer arithmetic can be legitimate in low-level C++ code, but it raises review difficulty.

8
00:00:39.651 --> 00:00:47.264
A calculation may be correct only if several earlier assumptions are true: the pointer belongs to the right object, the offset was checked, the

9
00:00:47.264 --> 00:00:51.387
resulting position is within the same range, and the lifetime has not ended.

10
00:00:51.387 --> 00:00:53.291
Those assumptions should not be invisible.

11
00:00:53.291 --> 00:00:55.828
Bounds and lifetime must be reasoned about together.

12
00:00:55.828 --> 00:01:03.758
A range that was valid yesterday is not valid after the container reallocated, the owner was destroyed, or a view outlived the data it observes.

13
00:01:03.758 --> 00:01:08.834
A safe interface should make ownership and borrowing relationships clear enough that callers can preserve them.

14
00:01:08.834 --> 00:01:13.275
Defensive code keeps range information attached to the data for as long as practical.

15
00:01:13.275 --> 00:01:20.253
Containers, spans, iterators with clear begin and end rules, and small domain objects can all carry more meaning than a pointer alone.

16
00:01:20.253 --> 00:01:25.329
The goal is to reduce places where size context is discarded and later reconstructed by hand.

17
00:01:25.329 --> 00:01:31.990
During review, look for function signatures that accept pointer-only parameters, pointer plus loosely named integer parameters, or offsets that are

18
00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:33.893
not tied to a visible range.

19
00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:39.920
Those are signs that the code may be depending on an unwritten contract rather than a checked, testable one.
